<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holy Land</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holy Land is the region between the Jordan River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, roughly ancient Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, held sacred by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and Muslims. Medieval Europeans used the term for the places of Christ's life, the goal of pilgrimage and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>. Venetian ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> served as stopovers for pilgrim ships bound there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-land.jpg" alt="Holy Land" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marino Sanuto the Elder (c. 1260 – 1338); Pietro Vesconte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_map_of_the_Holy_Land_by_Marino_Sanudo_(drawn_in_1320).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
